<p>
  Most businesses that fail do not fail because the product was bad. They fail
  because not enough people wanted it, and the person building it found that out
  after six months instead of after two days.
</p>

<p>
  So the first job is not building. It is finding out whether anybody has the
  problem you are about to solve, and whether they mind about it enough to hand
  over money.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">A problem beats an idea</h2>

<p>
  "An app for organizing homework" is an idea. "My mom spends an hour every
  Sunday driving my brother around and she hates it" is a problem. Problems are
  better because somebody is already annoyed, which means they are already
  looking for a way out.
</p>

<p>
  The strongest signal you can find is somebody who is <strong>already paying to
  solve it badly</strong>. If a neighbor already pays somebody to do a
  job you could do better, you do not have to convince them the job is worth
  paying for. That argument is already won.
</p>

<p>
  The second strongest is somebody doing it themselves and resenting it. The
  weakest is somebody who says "that sounds cool". People are polite. Politeness
  is not demand.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Three questions to ask about any idea</h2>

<ul class="m-0 pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
  <li>
    <strong>Who exactly?</strong> Not "people". Name five real humans you could
    message today. If you cannot name five, you do not know who this is for yet.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>What are they doing instead right now?</strong> Everything has a
    competitor, and usually the competitor is "nothing" or "doing it themselves".
    If the answer is "nothing, because they do not care", stop.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Why would they pick you?</strong> Cheaper, faster, closer, nicer to
    deal with, or simply available when nobody else is. Being younger is not a
    reason. Being reliable often is.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Test it before you build it</h2>

<p>
  The whole point of testing is to spend as little as possible finding out you
  were wrong. So do the smallest version first.
</p>

<ul class="m-0 pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
  <li>
    Do the job once, by hand, for one person. If you want to run a tutoring
    business, tutor one student. Everything you learn in that hour is worth more
    than a week of planning.
  </li>
  <li>
    Ask for the money. This is the test. Praise is free and means nothing.
    Somebody actually paying is the only proof that stands up.
  </li>
  <li>
    Do not buy equipment yet. Borrow it, rent it, or do the version that needs
    none. Buying a camera before your first paid shoot turns a hobby into a debt.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Narrow beats broad, every time</h2>

<p>
  "I do design" is hard to sell because nobody knows what to ask you for. "I make
  logos for small food businesses" is easy to sell, easy to recommend, and easy
  to get good at fast. You are not shrinking your market. You are becoming the
  obvious answer for one slice of it.
</p>

<p>
  You can always widen later. Almost every business that got big started
  ridiculously narrow, and the ones that started broad mostly never found anyone.
</p>

<div class="card">
  <p class="m-0 text-sm">
    <strong>Do this today:</strong> write one sentence in the form "I help
    <em>who</em> to <em>do what</em>". Then send it to five people who fit the
    first blank and ask what they currently do about it. Their answers will
    change your sentence, and that is the point.
  </p>
</div>
